      "note": "Built and twice-green. SealConversionRefused now sits beside SealDowngradeRefused at the store's one write door, so a measured seal landing over a standing `open` is refused unless `converting_because` rides with it, and the reason lands permanently in the surviving record's evidence because the store REPLACES. The CLI announces the override BEFORE the run when --netns disagrees with a proof's standing reading, reports a refusal on its own line, and carries the batch on. test_seal_is_never_silently_dropped.py runs 10 teeth and test_cli.py runs 10, all green on two consecutive runs. One self-contradicting clause was corrected while here: the file asserted `open` -> sealed was 'the first real seal of a proof that had never been asked', and its own fourth tooth already said nothing standing is not `open`. The reseal ladder is untouched — reseal.py:367 reproduces the standing isolation, so a standing `open` re-runs at `none` and lands open -> open.",
